\details{
Vocabulary (per species):

\preformatted{
{ACCESS|SPAWN|REAR|""} ; {NONE|DAM|MODELLED|ASSESSED|REMEDIATED} [;INTERMITTENT]
}

Token 1 (habitat) per bcfp:
\itemize{
\item \code{ACCESS}: species' barriers all upstream (i.e. accessible to
species) AND segment has no spawning AND no rearing eligibility
for the species
\item \code{SPAWN}: spawning eligibility > 0 (always wins over REAR)
\item \code{REAR}: rearing eligibility > 0 AND no spawning
\item \code{""} (empty): species has at least one downstream barrier blocking
it (so habitat label is suppressed -- inaccessible).
\item For species without rearing semantics (CM, PK), the rearing
conditions drop out -- only \code{ACCESS} (no spawn, no barriers) and
\code{SPAWN} (spawning > 0) emit.
}

Token 2 (barrier source) only emits when the species' barriers
downstream is empty (i.e. accessible). It comes from the bundle's
\code{parameters_mapping_code_rules.csv} (link#197): per residence, rows in
\code{precedence} order, first matching \code{when} wins, so a bundle adds or reorders
a token with no code change. A bundle that declares no file, or a call with
no \code{cfg}, reads the package copy, bcfishpass's order. Resident and
anadromous species differ there in their CASE order:
\itemize{
\item Resident:    REMEDIATED > DAM (the next barrier down is a dam) >
ASSESSED (anthropogenic + pscis + no dam) > MODELLED
(anthropogenic + no pscis + no dam) > NONE.
\item Anadromous:  REMEDIATED > DAM (any dam) > ASSESSED (any pscis) >
MODELLED (any anthropogenic) > NONE.
}

Token 3 emits \code{INTERMITTENT} when the segment's \code{feature_code} matches
the bcfp intermittent code (default \code{"GA24850150"}) AND the species'
barriers downstream is empty.

Empty / NULL tokens are dropped via \code{paste(..., collapse = ";")}-style
composition so an inaccessible segment yields \code{""} (empty string) and
an accessible no-habitat-no-intermittent segment yields \code{"ACCESS;NONE"}.
}
